enum OtpPurpose {
  PHONE_VERIFICATION
  PASSWORD_RESET
  DEVICE_VERIFICATION
  ACCOUNT_LINKING
  /// Passwordless sign-in. The primary way into the product: she types her
  /// number, we send a code, the code is the credential. A password, where she
  /// has opted into one, only ever *shortens* this on a device the platform
  /// already knows.
  LOGIN
  /// Proof of control over the number currently on the account, demanded
  /// before it can be moved. The other half of the pair is a
  /// `PHONE_VERIFICATION` code on the incoming number.
  PHONE_CHANGE
  /// Sent to the number on file when a support-mediated recovery case is
  /// approved, so the real owner — who is still holding her SIM in every
  /// scenario except the one recovery exists for — can stop it.
  ACCOUNT_RECOVERY

  /// Proof that a live human at the delivery number meant to place a
  /// cash-on-delivery order the trust engine was not sure about. It grants no
  /// session and verifies no number — it releases exactly one order.
  ORDER_CONFIRMATION
}

/// Q47/Q48 P1 — WhatsApp is the primary channel; SMS needs a local provider.
enum OtpChannel {
  WHATSAPP
  SMS
  EMAIL
}

/// Q47/Q48 P1 — WhatsApp is the declared primary channel, SMS through a local
/// Syrian aggregator is the fallback that reaches a handset with mobile data
/// switched off, voice is the last automated resort, and the ops console is a
/// closed-beta stopgap that a human reads out loud.
/// Which direction a one-time code travels.
///
/// `CODE_TO_HANDSET` is the classic rail: the platform mints six digits and a
/// transport carries them out to her. `REVERSE_SMS` turns the arrow round —
/// SYROTP mints the code, she sends it from her own handset to a receiver
/// number the platform owns, and the match between the inbound sender and the
/// number she claimed is the proof. Nothing is sent outbound, so the dominant
/// Syrian failure mode an aggregator produces — accepted, never delivered —
/// cannot occur on this mode at all.
///
/// It also closes the attack `matchCode` only ever mitigated: a code that
/// travels *from* her phone cannot be recited to her by somebody pretending to
/// be support, because the attacker would have to send the message from her
/// SIM.
enum OtpDeliveryMode {
  CODE_TO_HANDSET
  REVERSE_SMS
}

/// Which SYROTP ceremony a reverse challenge is waiting on.
///
/// The provider requires a `verified` phone-binding row for `(app, phone)`
/// before it will start a verification at all, and refuses with
/// `phone_not_bound` otherwise — there is no soft mode. So the first reverse
/// challenge a number ever sees is a `BINDING`, every later one is a
/// `VERIFICATION`, and both are settled by one inbound message whose sender is
/// checked. They prove the same thing, which is why a binding is allowed to
/// satisfy the challenge that opened it rather than asking her to send a
/// second message for no extra assurance.
enum ReverseCeremony {
  BINDING
  VERIFICATION
}

enum OtpTransportKind {
  WHATSAPP_CLOUD
  SMS_GATEWAY
  VOICE
  OPS_CONSOLE
}

/// `SENT` means the provider accepted the message. `DELIVERED` means a receipt
/// said it reached the handset. The gap between those two is the dominant
/// Syrian failure mode — accepted, never delivered — and is what failover has
/// to react to.
enum OtpDeliveryStatus {
  QUEUED
  SENDING
  SENT
  DELIVERED
  FAILED
  SKIPPED
  EXPIRED
}

enum OtpOutboxStatus {
